items.workedExampleMarkdown:
```js
const order = {
  id: 'o-1041',
  status: 'open',
  customer: { name: 'R. Vance', city: 'Marrickville' },
};

// 1. Changing a top-level field: safe.
const closed = { ...order, status: 'closed' };
closed.status;   // 'closed'
order.status;    // still 'open'  <- the caller's record survived

// 2. Changing a NESTED field through a shallow copy: not safe.
const moved = { ...order };
moved.customer.city = 'Ashfield';
order.customer.city;   // 'Ashfield'  <- the original changed too

// 3. Changing a nested field correctly: rebuild each level on the path.
const movedProperly = {
  ...order,
  customer: { ...order.customer, city: 'Ashfield' },
};
```

Case 2 is worth staring at. Nothing looks wrong: a copy was made, and only the copy was assigned to. But the copy's `customer` is the *same object* the original refers to, so there was only ever one city to change.

A quick way to keep this straight: a spread copies the values in the boxes, and for a nested object the value in the box is an arrow pointing elsewhere. Copying the arrow does not copy what it points at.

```js
const tickets = [
  { id: 'T-1', status: 'open', priority: 1, subject: 'Late delivery' },
  { id: 'T-2', status: 'closed', priority: 3, subject: 'Wrong item' },
  { id: 'T-3', status: 'open', priority: 2, subject: 'Refund query' },
];

// Which ones?  -> filter. Length may shrink.
const open = tickets.filter((ticket) => ticket.status === 'open');
open.length;     // 2
tickets.length;  // still 3

// What shape?  -> map. Length is always the same.
const rows = open.map((ticket) => ({ id: ticket.id, label: ticket.subject }));
rows.length;     // 2

// The trap: a new ARRAY of the SAME objects, edited.
const bad = tickets.map((ticket) => {
  ticket.priority = 1;   // edits the caller's ticket
  return ticket;
});
tickets[1].priority;     // 1  <- the source data changed

// The fix: new records, not edited ones.
const good = tickets.map((ticket) => ({ ...ticket, priority: 1 }));
```

Notice the shape of the arrow function in the good version: `({ ...ticket, ... })`. The parentheses are required, because a `{` at the start of an arrow body would otherwise begin a block rather than an object literal. Forgetting them gives a function that returns `undefined` for every element — a very recognisable array of `undefined`s.

```js
const tickets = [
  { id: 'T-1', status: 'open' },
  { id: 'T-2', status: 'closed' },
  { id: 'T-3', status: 'open' },
];

// Counting: the accumulator is an object built up key by key.
const counts = tickets.reduce((acc, ticket) => {
  acc[ticket.status] = (acc[ticket.status] || 0) + 1;
  return acc;
}, {});
// { open: 2, closed: 1 }

// Without the {} seed this throws on an empty array. With it, an empty
// list correctly produces {} and no caller needs a special case.
[].reduce((acc, t) => acc, {});   // {}
```

Now the lookup question. Given a list of tickets and a page that must show one ticket per row with its customer's name, scanning the customer list for every row is both slow and hard to read. Build a lookup first:

```js
const customers = [
  { id: 'c-1', name: 'R. Vance' },
  { id: 'c-2', name: 'J. Okoro' },
];

const byId = customers.reduce((acc, customer) => {
  acc[customer.id] = customer;
  return acc;
}, {});

byId['c-2'].name;        // 'J. Okoro'
byId['c-9'];             // undefined  <- a miss, and it is silent
```

That last line is the one to plan for. `byId['c-9'].name` throws; `byId['c-9']?.name` gives `undefined`; a deliberate default such as `byId[id] ?? { name: 'Unknown customer' }` says what should happen. Note `??` rather than `||`: the first only replaces `null` and `undefined`, so a legitimately falsy value survives.

